# Shopify/product-taxonomy

> Shopify's open product taxonomy, with attributes, values and mappings, for integrators and data scientists.

## The problem
Classifying products consistently across catalogs and marketplaces requires a shared vocabulary.

## What it actually does
The repository contains the source data in YAML (categories, attributes, values, return reasons), the translations and the mappings to other taxonomies, including Google. A Ruby application (`dev/`) validates this data and generates JSON and TXT files published in dated releases.

## How it is wired
Diagram based on the code diagram.
```mermaid
flowchart TD
  D["data/ (YAML)"] --> L["loader.rb"]
  L --> M["Taxonomy models"]
  M --> V["Validation (schémas CUE)"]
  M --> S["Serializers"]
  S --> X["dist/ et releases"]
  S --> Y["docs/ (Jekyll)"]
```

## Trying it
```bash
curl -L https://github.com/Shopify/product-taxonomy/releases/latest/download/categories.en.json.gz | gunzip > categories.json
cd dev
bundle install
bin/product_taxonomy dist
```

## Cost and gotchas
Free. The committed `dist/` folder is deprecated and will be removed on October 31, 2026: use the files from the releases. Ruby and cue are only required to modify the project.

## What it is not
Not an API: the contract is a set of files. Not an automatic product classifier.

## Alternatives
No alternative named in the README.

## For you
Worth watching: a good reference for labeling or normalizing product catalogs in a classification project.
